1. **Stable under inserts/deletes** — Offset pages skip or duplicate rows when new posts appear or old ones are removed while the client pages. A compound `(created_at, id)` cursor always continues from a known point in the sort order.
2. **Cheaper on large tables** — `OFFSET N` still walks/skips N rows; deep pages get slower. A keyed `WHERE` can use an index on `(created_at DESC, id DESC)` (or equivalent) and stay roughly constant-time.
3. **No expensive `COUNT(*)`** — Total counts force a full scan (or heavy aggregate work) on large `posts` tables. `hasMore` via `LIMIT N+1` is enough for infinite-scroll / “load more” UIs.
